  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1053 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge that dominated our weather for the last\nseveral days will shift eastward Friday, with a light southwest\nflow aloft developing across the area. That will support another \nvery warm day, with record or near record temperatures once again.\nThe forecast maximum at Denver is 86F, which would be just a \ndegree shy of the record (87F set in 1910). There will be some \nlingering mid level moisture, just enough for isolated high based \nshowers over the mountains, capable of producing gusty outflow \nwinds to 30-40 mph. \n\nSaturday will see stronger southwest flow aloft, resulting in\nanother unseasonably warm day and an increase in winds for the \nlate morning and afternoon hours. With that comes elevated to \nlocally critical fire weather conditions, but humidity increases \nacross the mountains along with a slight drop in temperatures. \nThe overlap of lowest humidity and breezy winds gusting to near 25\nmph will be over Washington and Lincoln Counties, but even there \nconditions are marginal for potential Red Flag. With slight \ncooling aloft, we should see a couple degrees of cooling except \nthe far northeast plains where downslope will support highs in the\nupper 80s/near 90 yet. \n\nFor Sunday to Monday, not a lot changes (or has changed in model\nsolutions) as the upper trough dives down the West Coast. Again,\nalmost all influence of this trough and the remnants of Hurricane\nRachel will stay well to our west. There is almost no chance of\nrain for the plains during this time, with only a chance of\npassing showers for the northern mountains. All the significant\nprecipitation will stay far to our west in the Desert Southwest\nand Great Basin. Temperatures will almost certainly stay well\nabove normal with highs in the lower 80s for the lower elevations,\npotentially cooling a bit Monday, still depending on a backdoor\ncold front.  \n\nOur main precipitation chances will have to wait until the upper \nlevel low ejects out of the southwest U.S. and across the Rockies.\nThere remains a great deal of uncertainty regarding the eventual \ntrack of this low, and thus who gets precipitation and when those\nhigher chances would occur. The latest trends seem to be favoring\nlocations north and east of our forecast area Tuesday night - \nThursday. However, there are still several ensemble members that \nshow a round of modest precipitation (30-40% chance of 0.5\" \nliquid with the odds favoring locations near the Wyoming border).\nEnsembles have also trended weaker with the trailing shortwave \ninto Thursday or Friday, which means a decreasing chance (already \nsmall) of any snowflakes into lower elevations. We do think the \ncurrent forecast track of the low would at least bring a few \ninches of snow to some of our northern mountains.",
